[section:eager Eager Operations]

The [link asio.reference.eager `eager`] completion token starts an asynchronous
operation immediately, and returns a handle whose result is awaited later. This
allows other work to proceed while the operation runs:

  auto op = asio::async_read(
      stream, asio::buffer(my_buffer),
      asio::eager);

  ... do other work while the read is going on ...

  std::move(op)(
      [](asio::error_code ec, std::size_t bytes_read)
      {
        ...
      });

The handle is move-only, and waiting consumes it, so the call operator must be
invoked on an rvalue. Any completion token may be used to await the result:

  std::future<std::size_t> f = std::move(op)(asio::use_future);

or, within a C++20 coroutine:

  auto [ec, n] = co_await std::move(op)(
      asio::as_tuple(asio::use_awaitable));

Within a C++20 coroutine the token may be omitted entirely, as for any other
asynchronous operation:

  std::size_t n = co_await std::move(op);

An eager operation is useful when the operation must be in flight while other
work proceeds:

  auto op = asio::async_read(stream, asio::buffer(my_buffer), asio::eager);

  co_await other.async_wait(asio::use_awaitable);

  auto [ec, n] = co_await std::move(op)(asio::as_tuple(asio::use_awaitable));

Written as a single expression, with nothing in between, `eager` behaves as
[link asio.overview.composition.deferred `deferred`] does, but allocates shared
state to do it. It is also useful where operations of different kinds must
share a type, as described below.

[heading Type Erasure]

An eager operation's type records the executor and the completion signatures,
and nothing else. The initiation and its arguments are held in the shared
state rather than in the type. Operations of different kinds share a single
type:

  typedef asio::eager_async_operation<
      void(asio::error_code, std::size_t)> read_operation;

  std::vector<read_operation> operations;
  operations.push_back(a.async_read_some(asio::buffer(buf1), asio::eager));
  operations.push_back(
      asio::async_read(b, asio::buffer(buf2, 4), asio::eager));

A leaf read and a composed read are distinct operations, yet both are stored
here as the same type. This makes it possible to keep in-flight operations in
a container, to pass one across an interface that is not a template, or to
return one from a virtual function.

A [link asio.overview.composition.deferred `deferred`] operation cannot do
this. Its type is
`deferred_async_operation<Signature, Initiation, InitArgs...>`, which encodes
the initiation and every initiation argument, so each operation is a distinct
type.

The executor is part of the type, so operations share a type when their I/O
objects share an executor type. Adapting an object to supply a default
completion token does not affect this. Such an executor differs from the one
it adapts only in the token it carries, and is not recorded in the type. An
object adapted with any token's `as_default_on_t` therefore yields the same
type as an unadapted one:

  asio::eager_t<>::as_default_on_t<tcp::socket> c(my_context);

  operations.push_back(c.async_read_some(asio::buffer(buf3)));

An executor that alters behaviour rather than carrying a default token, such
as a [link asio.reference.strand `strand`], is recorded in the type, as it
determines where the operation completes, as is a concrete executor type such
as `io_context::executor_type`. Operations on such objects are named using
[link asio.reference.basic_eager_async_operation `basic_eager_async_operation`].

They may still be held alongside the others, as an eager operation converts to
one whose executor type its own converts to. The shared state does not depend
on the executor, so the conversion moves the operation rather than copying or
reallocating it:

  asio::basic_stream_socket<tcp, asio::io_context::executor_type>
      d(my_context);

  operations.push_back(d.async_read_some(asio::buffer(buf4), asio::eager));

Here the operation's executor is `io_context::executor_type`, which converts
to `any_io_executor`, so the operation converts to `read_operation`.

[heading Piping]

Eager operations support piping. Since the handle is a value, a stage may be
applied at the point of awaiting rather than at the point of starting, which
bounds the remaining wait rather than the operation as a whole. A deferred
operation cannot express this, as it has not started yet:

  auto op = asio::async_read(stream, asio::buffer(my_buffer), asio::eager);

  co_await other.async_wait(asio::use_awaitable);

  // Five seconds from here, not from where the read started.
  auto [ec, n] = co_await (std::move(op)
      | asio::cancel_after(std::chrono::seconds(5),
          asio::as_tuple(asio::use_awaitable)));

The parentheses around the piped expression are required, as the operand of
`co_await` is a ['cast-expression] and `co_await x | y` would otherwise parse
as `(co_await x) | y`. The `std::move` is also required, as the pipe operator
takes its left operand by value and an eager operation is move-only.

[heading Detaching and Cancellation]

An eager operation whose result is no longer required may simply be discarded.
Discarding the handle detaches from the operation. The operation is not
cancelled, but continues to run, and its result is discarded when it completes.

[note Because a detached operation continues to run, any buffers or other
objects that it refers to must remain valid until it completes.]

Awaiting the operation passes ownership to the completion token. When the token
is a lazy one, such as [link asio.overview.composition.deferred `deferred`], the
resulting operation owns the eager operation in turn, and discarding it detaches
in the same way.

To stop an operation rather than detach from it, request cancellation
explicitly:

  op.cancel();

or, as for any other asynchronous operation, bind a cancellation slot to the
completion handler used to await the result:

  asio::cancellation_signal sig;

  std::move(op)(
      asio::bind_cancellation_slot(sig.slot(),
        [](asio::error_code ec, std::size_t bytes_read)
        {
          ...
        }));

  ...

  sig.emit(asio::cancellation_type::all);

The cancellation slot remains effective once the operation has been converted to
a lazy one, and so is the means of cancelling a chain that no longer has a
handle of its own.

To determine whether the result is already available, and can therefore be
taken without waiting, use:

  if (op.ready())
    ...

[heading Thread Safety]

An eager operation adds no thread safety to the operation that it wraps. A
cancellation request is emitted on the thread that calls `cancel`, or that emits
the cancellation signal, and is delivered to the underlying operation there.

It is the caller's responsibility to ensure that cancellation is requested from
a context that is safe with respect to the operation's execution, exactly as it
would be when cancelling that operation directly. If the operation
was launched on a strand, for example, the cancellation must be emitted from
that strand:

  asio::dispatch(my_strand,
      [&]
      {
        op.cancel();
      });

The operation's I/O object must also still exist when cancellation is requested.

[heading Idempotency]

The token is idempotent. Applying it to an operation that has already been
eagerly initiated yields that same operation, without starting a second one or
allocating further shared state:

  auto op = timer.async_wait(asio::eager)(asio::eager);

Consequently, an allocator specified on a subsequent application is unused, as
the shared state has already been allocated.

[heading Awaiting Several Operations]

Because an eager operation is itself an asynchronous operation, several of them
may be combined using [link asio.overview.composition.parallel_group parallel
groups]. As the operations have already been started, the group waits for
results that are already in flight. For example, to apply a timeout to a read:

  auto [order, timer_ec, read_ec, n] =
    co_await asio::experimental::make_parallel_group(
        timer.async_wait(asio::eager),
        asio::async_read(stream, asio::buffer(my_buffer), asio::eager)
      ).async_wait(
        asio::experimental::wait_for_one(),
        asio::use_awaitable);

  if (order[0] == 0)
  {
    // timed out
  }
  else
  {
    // completed in time
  }

[heading Eager as the Default Completion Token]

An I/O object may be adapted so that `eager` is its default completion token,
allowing the token to be omitted at each call site:

  using eager_tcp_socket = asio::eager_t<>::as_default_on_t<tcp::socket>;
  // ...
  void do_read(eager_tcp_socket my_socket)
  {
    auto op = my_socket.async_read_some(asio::buffer(my_buffer));
    // ...
    std::move(op)(
        [](asio::error_code ec, std::size_t bytes_read)
        {
          ...
        });
  }

